# `core/tasks`'s background half, driven the way a user drives it: a library
# with work in it, an application that opens one scope and spawns that work into
# it, and `buri test` — which on a toolchain with a native backend links a real
# binary and runs it.
#
# The conformance corpus holds `scope` and `spawn`'s semantics and holds them on
# every backend, and its build file says `platforms: [JS]` so that the reference
# run stays the reference one. What it cannot say is what a person gets: a
# package that exposes a `run` and cannot spawn it, an application that can, and
# one command. That is this case.
#
# The last step is the negative twin the corpus rule asks for. What is flipped
# is the scope's own promise — that everything spawned has finished by the time
# it returns — because that is the claim the whole feature is, and a green suite
# proves nothing about a failure mode nobody has seen fail.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "a scope waits for a socket loop and a timer, a handler spawns into it later, and the double picks the order"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/page", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "pass.txt"
}

# And again, from the cache: nothing about a scope is a fact about the machine.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/page"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "cached.txt"
}

# The negative twin. A scope returns when its body *and every task spawned into
# it* have finished, so the assertion that would hold if it returned early is
# the body's line alone. The report that records the difference is what says
# the waiting is real rather than asserted.
edit {
  file: "lib/page/test/feed.buri"
  replace: "        \"mounted\\nframe a\\nframe b\\nsocket closed\\nsessions expired at 300000\\n\","
  with: "        \"mounted\\n\","
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/page", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "refused.txt"
}
